1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,936
This is ECE 358, video 6, part a. The topic is DNS, the Domain Name System.

2
00:00:08,386 --> 00:00:16,087
After this video, you can explain the DNS services, and why DNS is a distributed hierarchy.

3
00:00:16,537 --> 00:00:21,977
You can draw the iterative and the recursive name resolution, message by message.

4
00:00:22,427 --> 00:00:28,614
You can explain caching, the TTL, resource records and the DNS message format.

5
00:00:29,064 --> 00:00:37,405
Part b, video 6 b, solves the DNS questions of the question bank, and problem 4 of problem set 2.

6
00:00:37,855 --> 00:00:48,863
Internet hosts and routers have two identifiers. The first is a name, for example ece.uwaterloo.ca. Humans use the name.

7
00:00:49,313 --> 00:01:03,329
The second is the IP address, a 32-bit number such as 129.97.56.100. The network uses the IP address to address datagrams.

8
00:01:03,779 --> 00:01:09,070
So we need a map from the name to the IP address. DNS gives this map.

9
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:18,544
DNS is a distributed database. It is a hierarchy of many name servers. No single server keeps all the names.

10
00:01:18,994 --> 00:01:29,213
DNS is also an application-layer protocol. Hosts and DNS servers exchange DNS messages to resolve names.

11
00:01:29,663 --> 00:01:40,479
This is a core Internet function, but it runs on hosts and DNS servers, not in the routers. The slides call this complexity at the network's edge.

12
00:01:40,929 --> 00:01:48,993
DNS gives four services. The first is hostname-to-IP-address translation. This is the main service.

13
00:01:49,443 --> 00:01:58,360
The second is host aliasing. A host has one canonical name: the true, official name. It can also have alias names.

14
00:01:58,810 --> 00:02:12,570
In the slide example, www.uwaterloo.ca is an alias, and uwaterloo.ca is the canonical name. DNS gives the canonical name for an alias.

15
00:02:13,020 --> 00:02:19,569
The third is mail server aliasing. DNS gives the name of the mail server of a domain.

16
00:02:20,019 --> 00:02:30,131
For example, Waterloo users have email addresses at uwaterloo.ca, but the mail server is in Office 365.

17
00:02:30,581 --> 00:02:36,171
The fourth is load distribution. A busy site has replicated Web servers.

18
00:02:36,621 --> 00:02:50,125
Many IP addresses correspond to one name. DNS can give different addresses to different clients, so the servers share the load. The name cnn.com is only an example.

19
00:02:50,575 --> 00:02:58,212
Why not keep all names in one central DNS server? Every host in the world would send its queries to it.

20
00:02:58,662 --> 00:03:04,593
First, it is a single point of failure. If this server fails, no host can resolve a name.

21
00:03:05,043 --> 00:03:13,533
Second, the traffic volume is too high. The Comcast DNS servers alone get 600 billion queries per day.

22
00:03:13,983 --> 00:03:21,429
One day has 24 times 60 times 60 seconds. That is 86,400 seconds.

23
00:03:21,879 --> 00:03:30,369
600 billion divided by 86,400 is about 6.94 million queries per second.

24
00:03:30,819 --> 00:03:40,910
The Akamai DNS servers alone get 2.2 trillion queries per day. That is about 25.5 million queries per second.

25
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:46,544
Third, a central database is far from most clients, so the delay is long.

26
00:03:46,994 --> 00:03:53,437
Fourth, one central database is hard to maintain, because every change goes to one place.

27
00:03:53,887 --> 00:04:00,031
So a central design does not scale. DNS is distributed and decentralized.

28
00:04:00,481 --> 00:04:13,473
Slide 32 gives the size. DNS has about one billion records, and it handles many trillions of queries per day. There are many more reads than writes, and milliseconds count.

29
00:04:13,923 --> 00:04:19,149
Millions of different organizations are each responsible for their own records.

30
00:04:19,599 --> 00:04:32,634
DNS is a hierarchy with three levels. At the top are the root DNS servers. There are 13 in total, named A to M. Each one is a cluster of many physical servers.

31
00:04:33,084 --> 00:04:42,983
Under the root are the top level domain servers, or TLD servers. There are servers for dot com, dot org, dot edu, and so on.

32
00:04:43,433 --> 00:04:56,531
Under the TLD servers are the authoritative DNS servers of each organization, such as the amazon.com and the umass.edu DNS servers.

33
00:04:56,981 --> 00:05:07,883
Now a client wants the IP address of www.amazon.com. The slide gives a first approximation with three queries.

34
00:05:08,333 --> 00:05:15,287
Query 1. The client queries a root server. The root server gives the dot com DNS server.

35
00:05:15,737 --> 00:05:23,951
Query 2. The client queries the dot com DNS server. It gives the amazon.com DNS server.

36
00:05:24,401 --> 00:05:35,793
Query 3. The client queries the amazon.com DNS server. It gives the IP address of www.amazon.com.

37
00:05:36,243 --> 00:05:39,251
Each level knows only the next level down.

38
00:05:39,701 --> 00:05:48,213
The root name servers are the contact of last resort. A name server that cannot resolve a name contacts a root server.

39
00:05:48,663 --> 00:05:52,481
There are 13 logical root servers, named A to M.

40
00:05:52,931 --> 00:06:02,787
Each logical server is replicated many times. There are about 1700 root instances, in 170 countries.

41
00:06:03,237 --> 00:06:16,016
ICANN manages the root DNS domain. DNSSEC gives security: origin authentication and message integrity. The Internet cannot work without the root servers.

42
00:06:16,466 --> 00:06:27,559
The top level domain servers are responsible for dot com, dot org, dot net, dot edu, and all country domains, such as dot ca and dot uk.

43
00:06:28,009 --> 00:06:32,233
Network Solutions is the registry for dot com and dot net.

44
00:06:32,683 --> 00:06:41,643
CIRA, in Ottawa, is responsible for dot ca. Its physical servers are in undisclosed cities around the world.

45
00:06:42,093 --> 00:06:50,989
The authoritative DNS servers belong to an organization. For example, Waterloo, Amazon and UMass have their own.

46
00:06:51,439 --> 00:06:59,631
They give the official answer for the named hosts of the organization. The organization or a service provider keeps them.

47
00:07:00,081 --> 00:07:10,279
Each ISP has a local DNS name server. When a host makes a DNS query, the query goes to its local DNS server.

48
00:07:10,729 --> 00:07:16,083
The local server first looks in its local cache of recent name-to-address pairs.

49
00:07:16,533 --> 00:07:21,675
If it finds the name, it replies at once. This answer can be out of date.

50
00:07:22,125 --> 00:07:31,874
If the name is not in the cache, the local server forwards the query into the DNS hierarchy. Then it returns the answer to the host.

51
00:07:32,324 --> 00:07:37,081
The local DNS server does not strictly belong to the hierarchy.

52
00:07:37,531 --> 00:07:48,070
To find your local DNS server, type scutil --dns on MacOS. On Windows, type ipconfig /all.

53
00:07:48,520 --> 00:08:02,173
How does the OS know the address of the local DNS server? This part is extra. At power on, the OS runs the DHCP protocol, and sends a DHCP request.

54
00:08:02,623 --> 00:08:17,429
The DHCP reply gives your IP address, the IP addresses of the local DNS servers, the subnet mask, and the default gateway. The network layer chapter explains DHCP.

55
00:08:17,879 --> 00:08:27,521
Now the details of name resolution. A DNS query has a header and a query part. The query part contains the domain name.

56
00:08:27,971 --> 00:08:35,502
The host sends the query to the IP address of its local DNS server, on UDP port 53.

57
00:08:35,952 --> 00:08:49,584
DNS has two kinds of queries. In a recursive query, the client asks the server, called the resolver, for the final answer. That is the IP address, not a referral to another server.

58
00:08:50,034 --> 00:08:56,477
To ask for recursion, the client sets the RD flag, recursion desired, to 1 in the header.

59
00:08:56,927 --> 00:09:08,788
A recursive reply has the RA flag, recursion available. The resolver can also decline. Then RA is 0, that is false, and the reply is a referral.

60
00:09:09,238 --> 00:09:20,438
In an iterative query, the server replies with the best information it has. That is a referral to another DNS server, or the IP address if it knows it.

61
00:09:20,888 --> 00:09:26,861
The client then queries the referred server, and repeats this until the name is resolved.

62
00:09:27,311 --> 00:09:33,029
A host almost always sends a recursive query to its local DNS server.

63
00:09:33,479 --> 00:09:53,191
Here is the first resolution example of slide 40. A host at ece.uwaterloo.ca wants the IP address of gaia.cs.umass.edu. This is the worst case: the local DNS server knows no server except a root server.

64
00:09:53,641 --> 00:10:07,081
Message 1. The host sends a recursive query to its local DNS server, dns.uwaterloo.ca, on UDP port 53. Blue arrows are queries.

65
00:10:07,531 --> 00:10:12,629
Message 2. The local server sends an iterative query to a root server.

66
00:10:13,079 --> 00:10:23,383
Message 3. The root server does not know the name. It replies with the address of the dot edu TLD server. Green arrows are replies.

67
00:10:23,833 --> 00:10:28,825
Message 4. The local server sends the query to the TLD server.

68
00:10:29,275 --> 00:10:40,454
Message 5. The TLD server replies with the name of the authoritative server of umass, dns.cs.umass.edu.

69
00:10:40,904 --> 00:10:45,213
Message 6. The local server queries the authoritative server.

70
00:10:45,663 --> 00:10:54,666
Message 7. The authoritative server replies with the IP address of gaia.cs.umass.edu.

71
00:10:55,116 --> 00:11:05,121
Message 8. The local server returns the IP address to the host. That is 8 messages in total: 4 queries and 4 replies.

72
00:11:05,571 --> 00:11:20,014
The queries from the local server are iterated queries. Each contacted server replies with the name of the next server to contact: I do not know this name, but ask this server. The local server does the work.

73
00:11:20,464 --> 00:11:29,019
Slide 41 shows the same name with full recursion. This is a hypothetical example. It is not done in practice.

74
00:11:29,469 --> 00:11:34,759
Message 1 is the same recursive query from the host to the local server.

75
00:11:35,209 --> 00:11:40,265
Message 2. The local server sends a recursive query to the root server.

76
00:11:40,715 --> 00:11:46,198
Message 3. The root server sends a recursive query to the TLD server.

77
00:11:46,648 --> 00:11:51,853
Message 4. The TLD server sends it on to the authoritative server.

78
00:11:52,303 --> 00:11:58,682
Message 5. The authoritative server returns the IP address to the TLD server.

79
00:11:59,132 --> 00:12:03,740
Message 6. The TLD server returns it to the root server.

80
00:12:04,190 --> 00:12:08,414
Message 7. The root server returns it to the local server.

81
00:12:08,864 --> 00:12:15,861
Message 8. The local server gives the IP address to the host. Again, there are 8 messages.

82
00:12:16,311 --> 00:12:27,426
A recursive query puts the burden of name resolution on the contacted name server. Here the root server must keep the state of each query and wait for the answer.

83
00:12:27,876 --> 00:12:39,247
With trillions of queries, the upper levels of the hierarchy get a very heavy load. So the root and TLD servers answer with referrals, as in example 1.

84
00:12:39,697 --> 00:12:53,606
Name resolution takes many messages. So DNS uses caching. When any name server learns a mapping, it caches the mapping. It then answers the next query for this name at once, from the cache.

85
00:12:54,056 --> 00:13:12,616
Here is an example with added numbers, not from the slides. The RTT from the host to the local server is 2 milliseconds. From the local server, the RTT to a root server is 40, to the TLD server 30, and to the authoritative server 50.

86
00:13:13,066 --> 00:13:19,658
With an empty cache, the local server must ask the root, the TLD and the authoritative server.

87
00:13:20,108 --> 00:13:27,127
The response time is 2 plus 40 plus 30 plus 50. That is 122 milliseconds.

88
00:13:27,577 --> 00:13:34,702
Local servers typically cache the addresses of the TLD servers. Then the root server is not needed.

89
00:13:35,152 --> 00:13:40,357
The time drops to 2 plus 30 plus 50. That is 82 milliseconds.

90
00:13:40,807 --> 00:13:49,853
If the local server has the name itself in its cache, the answer takes only 2 milliseconds. So caching improves the response time.

91
00:13:50,303 --> 00:14:04,148
Cache entries do not stay forever. Each entry has a time to live, the TTL, for example 3600 seconds. After the TTL, the entry times out and disappears.

92
00:14:04,598 --> 00:14:17,441
Cached entries can be out of date. Say that the named host changes its IP address 1200 seconds after the entry was cached. The cached copy keeps the old address.

93
00:14:17,891 --> 00:14:27,789
So the cached entry is stale for 3600 minus 1200 seconds. That is 2400 seconds.

94
00:14:28,239 --> 00:14:38,074
So a change may not be known Internet-wide until all TTLs expire. DNS gives best-effort name-to-address translation.

95
00:14:38,524 --> 00:14:48,828
The DNS database stores resource records, or RRs. Each RR has four fields: name, TTL, type and value.

96
00:14:49,278 --> 00:14:52,905
The type field sets the meaning of the name and of the value.

97
00:14:53,355 --> 00:14:58,965
Type A, for address. The name is a hostname, and the value is its IP address.

98
00:14:59,415 --> 00:15:14,029
For example, relay1.bar.foo.com has the address 145.37.93.126, with a TTL of 3600 seconds.

99
00:15:14,479 --> 00:15:23,567
Type NS, for name server. The name is a domain. The value is the hostname of the authoritative name server for this domain.

100
00:15:24,017 --> 00:15:31,611
For example, the authoritative name server of the domain foo.com is dns.foo.com.

101
00:15:32,061 --> 00:15:39,229
Type CNAME, for canonical name. The name is an alias. The value is the canonical, real name.

102
00:15:39,679 --> 00:15:45,183
Here foo.com is an alias for relay1.bar.foo.com.

103
00:15:45,633 --> 00:15:56,599
Another CNAME example from the slide: www.ibm.com is really servereast.backup2.ibm.com.

104
00:15:57,049 --> 00:16:04,430
Type MX, for mail exchange. The value is the name of the SMTP mail server for the name.

105
00:16:04,880 --> 00:16:10,405
Here mail.bar.foo.com is the mail server for foo.com.

106
00:16:10,855 --> 00:16:16,807
DNS query and reply messages have the same format. First comes the header.

107
00:16:17,257 --> 00:16:20,692
The header has six fields. Each field is 2 bytes.

108
00:16:21,142 --> 00:16:25,003
So the header is 6 times 2 bytes. That is 12 bytes.

109
00:16:25,453 --> 00:16:28,973
After the header come four sections of variable length.

110
00:16:29,423 --> 00:16:41,370
The identification field is a 16-bit number for the query. The reply to this query uses the same number. So the client can match each reply to its query.

111
00:16:41,820 --> 00:16:54,940
The flags field says if the message is a query or a reply. It also has the recursion desired flag, the recursion available flag, and a flag that says the reply is authoritative.

112
00:16:55,390 --> 00:16:59,059
The next four fields count the entries in the four sections.

113
00:16:59,509 --> 00:17:11,861
The questions section has the name and type fields for a query. For example, ece.uwaterloo.ca with type A, or uwaterloo.ca with type MX.

114
00:17:12,311 --> 00:17:20,034
The answers section has the RRs in response to the query, each with name, TTL, type and value.

115
00:17:20,484 --> 00:17:24,345
The authority section has records for authoritative servers.

116
00:17:24,795 --> 00:17:41,051
The additional info section has helpful records that the client may use. For example, for an MX query, it contains the type A RR with the IP address of the mail server. Then the client needs no second query.

117
00:17:41,501 --> 00:17:56,392
Slide 46 shows how a new company enters the DNS. A new startup, Network Utopia, registers the name networkutopia.com at a DNS registrar, such as Network Solutions.

118
00:17:56,842 --> 00:18:03,647
It gives the names and IP addresses of its authoritative name servers, primary and secondary.

119
00:18:04,097 --> 00:18:20,140
The registrar inserts two RRs into the dot com TLD server. First, an NS record: the authoritative server for networkutopia.com is dns1.networkutopia.com.

120
00:18:20,590 --> 00:18:32,217
Second, an A record: dns1.networkutopia.com has the address 212.212.212.1.

121
00:18:32,667 --> 00:18:41,755
Then the company creates its authoritative server locally, at 212.212.212.1.

122
00:18:42,205 --> 00:18:49,287
It adds a type A record for its web server, www.networkutopia.com.

123
00:18:49,737 --> 00:18:56,137
It adds a type MX record for networkutopia.com, with the name of its mail server.

124
00:18:56,587 --> 00:19:09,814
Now anybody can resolve www.networkutopia.com. The dot com TLD server gives the NS record and the A record of dns1.

125
00:19:10,264 --> 00:19:14,360
Then dns1 gives the A record of the web server.

126
00:19:14,810 --> 00:19:25,733
Here is the recap. One. DNS maps names to IP addresses. It also gives host aliasing, mail server aliasing and load distribution.

127
00:19:26,183 --> 00:19:36,615
Two. DNS is a distributed hierarchy: root servers, TLD servers and authoritative servers. A central server does not scale.

128
00:19:37,065 --> 00:19:50,035
Three. A host sends a recursive query to its local DNS server, on UDP port 53. The local server answers from its cache, or else it asks the hierarchy.

129
00:19:50,485 --> 00:20:02,475
Four. A recursive query asks for the final answer. An iterative query gets a referral or the IP address. Both examples on the slides use 8 messages.

130
00:20:02,925 --> 00:20:08,962
Five. Caching with a TTL makes answers faster, but cached entries can be stale.

131
00:20:09,412 --> 00:20:22,895
Six. A resource record has a name, TTL, type and value. The four types are A, NS, CNAME and MX. A DNS message has a 12-byte header and four sections.

132
00:20:23,345 --> 00:20:40,561
Seven. For a new domain, the registrar puts NS and A records into the TLD server. The authoritative server holds the A, MX and CNAME records. That is the end of video 6, part a. Part b solves the exam questions.
